<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manisa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Magnesia ad Sipylum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manisa is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, at the foot of Mount Sipylus above the Gediz plain, north-east of Izmir. Known in antiquity as Magnesia ad Sipylum, it gave its name to the battle of 190 BCE in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saruhan-bey">Saruhan Bey</a> took it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in 1313 and made it the capital of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a>, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> it was a residence of princes being trained to govern.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manisa.jpg" alt="Manisa" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dosseman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manisa_Muradiye_Camii_Muradiye_Camii_side_to_southwest_6124.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Manisa lies about 40 kilometres north-east of İzmir, with its historic quarter on the forested slopes of Mount Sipylus beside the Çaybaşı Stream. Near it are the rock called Niobe's Weeping Rock and an ancient bridge, the Red Bridge. The Gediz plain, formed by river deposits, stretches out below. Ancient writers called the city Magnesia ad Sipylum to tell it from Magnesia on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Maeander</a>. Pliny the Elder says a notable city called Tantalis, the city of Tantalus, once stood on the same spot, and its ruins were still visible in his time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cybele">Cybele</a> monument at Akpınar, on the northern flank of Mount Sipylus, is among the evidence of Hittite influence in the region of the Luwian kingdom of Arzawa. Figures of tradition such as Tantalus, Pelops and Niobe are tied to the mountain. In the early seventh century BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydians">Lydians</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mermnad-dynasty">Mermnad dynasty</a> ruled much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, 62 kilometres inland.</p><p>Under Roman rule the city became important after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magnesia">Battle of Magnesia</a> in 190 BCE, was nearly destroyed by an earthquake in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>, and was restored by him. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> took it in 1076, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> recovered it after the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorylaeum">Dorylaeum</a> in 1097. During the Empire of Nicea, from 1204 to 1261, it held the imperial mint and treasury and was in effect the capital.</p><p>Turkish raids in the late 13th and early 14th centuries drove most inhabitants away after a failed campaign in 1302 by Michael IX. In 1313 Manisa fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">Beylik</a> of Saruhan, whose founder reigned until 1346, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> absorbed it in 1410, after an interval following the Battle of Ankara. In a practice begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a> in 1437, fifteen members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-dynasty">Ottoman dynasty</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> and Süleyman I, governed the Saruhan district in seventeen near-continuous periods until 1595. The princes there gained experience of government, and the city is still known as the city of shahzades. A severe earthquake in 1595 dealt a heavy blow to its prosperity.</p><p>Manisa was one of the first Ottoman cities served by a railway, the Smyrna Cassaba line, which reached Kasaba in 1866. The Greek army occupied it on 26 May 1919 and left on 8 September 1922; most of the city was burned as the army retreated. Manisa was rebuilt, and its name replaced Saruhan for the province and the city in 1927.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manisa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Saruhan Bey, a Turkmen commander, took <b>Magnesia</b> (Manisa) from the Byzantines in 1313 and made it the capital of a beylik in the Hermus valley, north of the Aydinids.</blockquote><a href="/state/saruhanids">Saruhanids</a></li></ul></section>
